+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a value to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of
+seconds, such as `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and only in that order, with no spaces, such as `2d`,
+`1h30m`, `90m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Units are case-insensitive. Negative numbers, decimals, unknown units, and repeated
+or out-of-order units are rejected. In help output the value is shown as `DURATION`.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+```
+
